# A here-document whose FIRST body line is ` EOF` — the delimiter spelling but with
# ONE leading space (POSIX §2.7.4). For a plain `<<` heredoc a delimiter line must
# equal the delimiter with NO leading blanks, so ` EOF` is ordinary CONTENT, not the
# closer; the heredoc closes only at the later bare `EOF` at column 0. (Pre-fix the
# leading space was eaten at the body boundary, `EOF\n` then matched the delimiter,
# and the heredoc CLOSED EARLY — `real` and the real `EOF` became stray commands and
# a second/third CommandLine appeared.) Here there is exactly ONE HeredocBody holding
# BOTH the ` EOF` line and the `real` line, closing on the column-0 `EOF`.
<<<cmd
printf 'cat <<EOF\n EOF\nreal\nEOF\n' | "$TP" run std.shell --input - --dump-tree
<<<assert
exit 0
out-absent ERROR
out-absent MISSING
out-count 1 CommandLine [
out-count 1 HeredocBody [
out-count 2 HeredocContentLine
out-order HeredocContentLine [10-15] ~ HeredocContentLine [15-20] ~ end: HeredocDelimLine [20-24]
